WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.135
In Taiwan, martial law was in place for a very long time.

00:00:03.494 --> 00:00:06.066
People's lives were subject to many restrictions.

00:00:06.538 --> 00:00:09.622
On nighttime streets, pedestrians always hurried home.

00:00:09.622 --> 00:00:13.759
In 1987, Taiwan announced the lifting of martial law.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:17.450
As the news spread, people felt new hope.

00:00:17.899 --> 00:00:21.461
People listened to the announcement on the radio again and again.

00:00:21.853 --> 00:00:24.827
Newspapers and magazines began to discuss more topics.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:28.140
Political and social restrictions gradually eased.

00:00:28.641 --> 00:00:31.679
Civic participation grew, and voices became more diverse.

00:00:32.168 --> 00:00:34.832
Everyone began to discuss public affairs openly.

00:00:35.287 --> 00:00:37.725
Discussions in parks often lasted until evening.

00:00:38.618 --> 00:00:41.062
The pace of democratization grew faster and faster.

00:00:41.758 --> 00:00:44.362
Young people also actively cared about society's future.

00:00:44.895 --> 00:00:47.705
New organizations sprang up, full of vitality.

00:00:47.705 --> 00:00:50.849
The lifting of martial law was an important step in Taiwan's democratic development.

00:00:51.272 --> 00:00:53.400
The air of freedom entered everyday life.
